lmn-gpo (1.6.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX: the shipped PowerShell scripts did not parse on Windows PowerShell 5.1. Without a
    byte-order mark, powershell.exe reads a .ps1 in the system codepage, so a UTF-8 em-dash
    (E2 80 94) decoded to a curly closing quote under CP1252 and ENDED the enclosing string
    early -- producing a cascade of "missing )" / "string is missing the terminator" parse
    errors and aborting the script. lmn-gpo-check.ps1 was unusable, and
    bootorder-pxe-first.ps1 carried the same characters inside strings, so the boot-order
    startup script was affected on clients too.
    All scripts/*.ps1 (and the generator in lmn_gpo/wlan.py) are now pure ASCII, which makes
    them independent of the client's codepage entirely.
  * The release workflow now fails if any shipped .ps1 contains a non-ASCII character, so
    this cannot regress.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.6.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New automatic prerequisite check. Applier.preflight() resolves every security-filter
    token (filter_deny, filter_deny_read, filter_apply) against the directory before
    anything is written, and reports each one that matches no group.
    - `lmn-gpo doctor` evaluates it against the real site.yaml, names every affected pack
      and school, and now exits 1 when an EXCLUSION resolves to nothing. That is the
      dangerous direction: the GPO then applies to exactly the devices or users it was
      meant to spare. A failed `filter_apply` is reported as a warning instead, because it
      fails closed (the pack is skipped).
    - `lmn-gpo apply` prints the same block BEFORE the first change, so a typo in
      teachernb or a school without a d_nopxe group surfaces up front rather than in the
      per-pack output after the fact.
    - `lmn-gpo env` flags any school that has no noPXE group at all.
    This replaces the manual "check lmn-gpo env before rolling out" step.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.5.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * SECURITY/DATA-LOSS FIX (regression in 1.3.0/1.4.0): `apply` refused nothing when the
    answers file was missing, mistyped or empty. load_site() returns {} for a non-existent
    path, so every optional package read as disabled and _retire() unlinked and DELETED its
    GPO -- up to 29 GPOs on a three-school site, without backup and with exit code 0.
    `apply` now aborts with exit 2 unless the answers file exists and is non-empty; the
    deliberate "no optional features" run needs the new --defaults flag.
  * _retire: take a `samba-tool gpo backup` into /var/backups/lmn-gpo/<timestamp>-<name>
    before deleting, and never retire on a precondition that depends on a FILE rather than
    an operator answer. `requires: wallpaper` is now reported and skipped instead of
    deleting the branding GPO -- the source checkout and the .deb use different wallpaper
    directories, so the documented upgrade path silently destroyed it.
  * Exclusions no longer fail open silently: when filter_deny or filter_deny_read resolves
    to no group (missing d_nopxe, typo in teachernb), apply prints a loud warning naming the
    pack and token and exits non-zero. Previously nothing was written and nothing was said,
    so the GPO reached exactly the devices it was meant to spare.
  * Global packs are linked domain-wide at OU=SCHOOLS, so their exclusion groups are now
    resolved against ALL detected schools, not only the ones selected for the run. Before,
    `apply --school a` left school b's teacher notebooks unexcluded while still applying.
  * Wi-Fi: both packages now follow `teachernb` instead of hard-wiring @nopxe, so a custom
    teacher-notebook group is honoured for Wi-Fi as it already was for power/lock/proxy.
    13-wlan-psk additionally carries filter_deny_read: ['@role-student'].
    NOTE: that denies students read on the GPO object only. sysvolreset writes a fixed ACL
    template, so the startup script FILE containing the passphrase stays readable by
    Authenticated Users. Documented in the pack and the README; the PSK must be treated as
    obtainable by students. Pre-staging the profile in the LINBO image avoids this.
  * setup: write site.yaml as 0600 in a 0700 directory (it holds plaintext Wi-Fi PSKs and
    the Veyon bind password); previously 0644.
  * apply: escape RFC 4515 specials in the custom teachernb group lookup, so a CN containing
    '*' or parentheses cannot silently match the wrong group or nothing at all.
  * Power: the display no longer switches off on its own. The timeout is now the site.yaml
    key display_off_seconds and defaults to 0 = never, so a machine driving a beamer or an
    interactive panel does not go dark mid-lesson. Set it to a number of seconds to restore
    a timeout. The screen still LOCKS after 30 min (pack 04-sperren) — the display then
    shows the lock screen instead of going dark; those are two separate settings.
  * gpp: use a generic NetBIOS name in a docstring example instead of a real customer one.
  * lmn-gpo-check.ps1: distinguish all-user from per-user Wi-Fi profiles. Only an all-user
    profile connects before login; a per-user one (a manual connect from the Wi-Fi flyout)
    is now reported as a failure instead of OK.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Proxy: exclude teacher notebooks (teachernb, by default the d_nopxe device group) from
    all five 12-proxy-* packages. They roam out of the school network, where the school proxy
    is unreachable; because the proxy is written to the real WinINET key rather than a
    Policies branch it tattoos, so an excluded notebook would otherwise stay pointed at an
    unreachable proxy and lose internet access off-site.
  * New catalog field filter_deny_read plus GpoEngine.deny_read. For the user-scope role
    proxy packages a plain deny-apply would be a silent no-op: those settings only reach a
    user through loopback processing, where Apply Group Policy is evaluated against the USER
    while (since MS16-072) the GPO list is retrieved in the COMPUTER's context. A device
    group can therefore only be excluded by denying it Read, so the machine never retrieves
    the GPO. The computer-scope packages (12-proxy-base, 12-proxy-zone-schule) use the
    ordinary filter_deny.
  * Note: removing the policy does not clear an already-provisioned client, because the
    WinINET proxy values tattoo. Reset affected profiles once, e.g. by deleting ProxyEnable
    under HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Internet Settings.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New pack 09b-kms-office: KMS activation for volume-licensed Microsoft Office. Office is
    NOT covered by pack 09-kms — it reads its own registry key
    (HKLM\Software\Microsoft\OfficeSoftwareProtectionPlatform), so the Windows setting never
    activated it. Covers Office LTSC 2024/2021, 2019 and 2016 (MSI and Click-to-Run, incl.
    Project/Visio); Microsoft 365 Apps is subscription-licensed and is skipped automatically.
    There is no ADMX policy for this: the official Office templates contain no KMS setting.
  * New site.yaml keys kms_office_host (empty = use kmshost, the usual one-server case),
    kms_office_port, and kms_port for the Windows side. The wizard asks for both hosts and
    now accepts and validates "host" or "host:port" — previously a typed-in "host:1689" was
    written verbatim into KeyManagementServiceName while the port stayed 1688, silently
    breaking the KMS client.
  * New startup script kms-activate-office.ps1: activates Office via CIM
    (SoftwareLicensingProduct, Office ApplicationId 0ff1ce15-...), not via ospp.vbs, which
    always exits 0 and can raise a modal dialog in session 0. Language-neutral, throttled,
    probes TCP/1688 first, and filters on the VOLUME_KMSCLIENT channel so it can never fire
    an online activation at Microsoft.
  * apply: fix a real cleanup gap — a pack whose precondition was removed (e.g. emptying
    kmshost) was only skipped, so its GPO stayed linked and kept pushing the OLD value.
    Such GPOs are now unlinked and deleted. Note the KMS values live outside the Policies
    branches and therefore tattoo: clearing them on an already-provisioned client needs
    slmgr.vbs /ckms resp. ospp.vbs /remhst.
  * lmn-gpo-check.ps1: add an Office activation section (CIM-based, read-only).

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 08:40:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.2.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New pack 18-point-and-print: allow standard users (students) to install printer
    drivers from the trusted print server(s) via Point and Print. Patched Windows 11
    otherwise blocks this after the PrintNightmare hardening (CVE-2021-34527), so the
    printers that linuxmuster/sophomorix already connects fail with "administrator
    required". Registry-only, opt-in (pointandprint_enabled); the trusted-server list is
    auto-detected (server NetBIOS name + FQDN + IP) plus printservers_extra. The printer
    connection itself is left to linuxmuster/sophomorix.
  * env: expose the server's machine NetBIOS name (server_netbios) so the trusted list
    matches how sophomorix connects (\\SERVER\..).
  * lmn-gpo-check.ps1: add a Point and Print client check section.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Mon, 13 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Rename for consistency with the lmn-gpo command: the Python module is now
    lmn_gpo (was lmgpo), the source launcher is lmn-gpo-cli, and the client check
    script is lmn-gpo-check.ps1.
  * Config directory moved to /etc/linuxmuster/lmn-gpo/ (was /etc/linuxmuster/lmgpo/).
    An existing site.yaml at the old path is migrated on install and is still read
    from a source checkout as a fallback — no settings are lost. Once the migration
    copy has succeeded, the old /etc/linuxmuster/lmgpo/site.yaml is removed.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <kevin@ks98.de>  Mon, 13 Jul 2026 13:00:00 +0000

lmn-gpo (1.0.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian package of the linuxmuster-gpo-template toolkit.
  * Installs the lmn-gpo CLI (/usr/bin/lmn-gpo), the lmgpo Python module and the
    read-only policy data under /usr/share/lmn-gpo (catalog, scripts, lib).
  * Reads its configuration from /etc/linuxmuster/lmgpo/site.yaml; an existing
    site.yaml from a previous source checkout is migrated on install.

